## Detailed information you can use right away

### 1️⃣ Stanley Park  

| Category | Details |
|----------|---------|
| **Address / Area** | Stanley Park, Vancouver, BC V6G 1Z4, Canada (the park surrounds downtown and borders the Burrard Inlet). |
| **How to get there** | **Public transit:** SkyTrain Canada Line → Waterfront Station, then a 10‑minute walk north along the waterfront. <br>**Bus:** #19 Stanley Park, #4 Dunbar, #44 U‑B‑C. <br>**Car:** Parking lots at Second Beach, English Bay, or the main gate (pay‑by‑hour). |
| **Top things to do** | • **Seawall walk / bike ride** – 9 km, flat, stroller‑friendly. <br>• **Vancouver Aquarium** – marine‑life shows, touch‑tanks, and a “Penguin Plunge” (kids love it). <br>• **Totem Poles at Brockton Point** – the most photographed Indigenous art in Canada. <br>• **Beaches** – English Bay (great for sunset) and Second Beach (playground, splash‑pad). <br>• **Lost Lagoon** – feeding ducks & swans (bring birdseed). |
| **Family‑friendly amenities** | • Multiple playgrounds (Second Beach, Stanley Park Playground). <br>• Picnic tables & BBQ sites (reserve online for larger groups). <br>• **Mini‑Train** (seasonal, 15‑minute ride). <br>• Bike‑rental stations (Spokes Bicycle Rentals, near the seawall). |
| **What to pack** | Comfortable walking shoes, light rain jacket (Pacific coast is misty in November), sunscreen, reusable water bottle, and a small blanket for a beach picnic. |
| **Nearby dining** | • **The Teahouse** (inside the park, near English Bay) – casual Canadian fare. <br>• **Stanley Park Bistro** – coffee, pastries, and light lunches. <br>• **Granville Island Public Market** (short bus ride) for a wider food selection. |
| **Tips for November** | • Days are shorter (sunset ≈ 4:30 pm). Plan the seawall walk early, then hit the aquarium later. <br>• The park’s foliage stays green through early November, and the mist adds a magical feel. <br>• If it rains, the indoor aquarium and the totem‑pole area are great backup activities. |

---

### 2️⃣ Capilano Suspension Bridge Park  

| Category | Details |
|----------|---------|
| **Address** | 3735 Capilano Rd, North Vancouver, BC V7R 4J1, Canada |
| **How to get there** | **Car:** 15‑minute drive north of downtown; follow signs “Capilano Suspension Bridge Park.” Parking $5‑$10 /hr (free for the first 30 min). <br>**Bus:** #236 “Capilano Bridge” from **Lions Gate Station** (SkyTrain). The bus drops you at the park entrance. <br>**Shuttle:** Some downtown hotels offer a private shuttle (check with your accommodation). |
| **Main experiences** | • **Capilano Suspension Bridge** – 137 m long, 70 m high, swaying over the river. <br>• **Treetops Adventure** – 12 suspended bridges woven among ancient cedars (great for photos). <br>• **Cliffwalk** – glass‑floor walkway clinging to a granite cliff (thrilling for older kids). <br>• **Indigenous Cultural Centre** – storytelling, art displays, and occasional drumming performances. <br>• **Kids’ Adventure** – a mini‑bridge, interactive stations, and a “rain‑forest explorer” trail. |
| **Family‑friendly amenities** | • Stroller‑accessible paths on the main bridge (though the treetop bridges have steps). <br>• Changing tables and family‑friendly restrooms near the entrance. <br>• On‑site café (Capilano Café) with kid‑friendly meals and hot chocolate. |
| **What to pack** | Closed‑toe shoes with good grip, light waterproof jacket, reusable water bottle, and a small backpack for snacks. |
| **Nearby dining** | • **Capilano Café** (on‑site) – sandwiches, soups, salads, and kid‑friendly options. <br>• **The Loon’s Nest** (a short drive in North Vancouver) for a sit‑down meal with a view of the river. |
| **Tips for November** | • The park closes at 5 pm (earlier on very short days). Arrive by 10 am to enjoy the bridge in the best light and avoid the late‑afternoon crowd. <br>• The rainforest stays damp; a rain jacket will keep you comfortable. <br>• Purchase tickets **online** (15 % discount) and print the QR code – you’ll skip the ticket line. <br>• If you have a family of 4, the **Family Pass** (2 adults + 2 children) saves about CAD 30. |
| **Optional add‑on** | **Grouse Mountain** is a 15‑minute drive from the park and offers a skyride, wildlife refuge, and spectacular city views – a good follow‑up if you have an extra half‑day. |

---

## How to fit them into your November 10‑20 itinerary  

| Day | Morning | Mid‑day / Afternoon | Evening |
|-----|---------|----------------------|---------|
| **Day 1** (or any day with good weather) | **Stanley Park** – start at Coal Harbour, walk the seawall to Second Beach (≈ 2 hrs). | Picnic lunch at Second Beach, then head to the **Vancouver Aquarium** (≈ 2 hrs). | Dinner in **Gastown** (e.g., The Flying Pig) – short walk back to downtown. |
| **Day 2** (or any other day) | **Capilano Suspension Bridge Park** – arrive by 10 am (take the bus 236 or drive). | Explore the bridge, treetops, cliffwalk, and Indigenous centre (≈ 2‑3 hrs). | Return to downtown; optional early dinner at **Lonsdale Quay** (great waterfront view) or head to **Grouse Mountain** for sunset views. |

Both attractions are **family‑friendly, highly rated, and easy to reach from downtown Vancouver**, making them perfect highlights for a ten‑day family vacation.  

